e  nanogram per mil levels and the standard  curve that's generated with the control  provided in the kit these simpie  sandwich Eliza does allow you a lot of  flexibility in choosing while where you  would like the antibodies to bind that  are responsible for a capture and  detection it can also allow you to look  at these analytes and various types of  matrices the sensitivity and specificity  now that you're using two  antibodies can be tremendous as  particularly the specificity aspect it  does require you to get a very nicely  matched pair of antibodies that will not  inhibit one another  I know internally this is something we  spend a lot of time working on is trying  to ensure that we get the sensitivity  and specificity you want with at the end  with antibodies that will not interfere  with each other's binding and it also  requires therefore that your analyte is  sufficiently large and accommodating  enough to have two or more of such  binding sites so that you can get clear  recognition in the sandwich itself so  now I'm going to talk about a relatively  new development in Sandwich eliza's that  is currently simple step iliza that by  adding yet another antibody makes the  whole process much more efficient and  quicker to use and it's a three-step  process and you will have your Eliza  result in 90 minutes post sample prep if  sample prep takes you an hour so then  you're looking at two and a half hours  for running the entire Eliza s a much  more rapid than the standard type of  sandwich Eliza in this sandwich Eliza  the capture antibody at the bottom of  the plate is specific to a tag and this  tag is now found on the capture antibody  for the analyte so you add the capture  antibody the analyte matrix itself and  the detector antibody conjugated to HR P  these are all mixed simultaneously  together in a single step incubation and  the anti tagged antibody pulls out the  entire complex the rest is washed off  and the teller development takes place  there